      "text": "To determine the number of different settings for a suitcase lock with 3 dials, each requiring a unique digit from 0 to 9, we need to calculate the number of permutations of 10 digits taken 3 at a time.\n\nThe number of permutations of 10 digits taken 3 at a time is given by the formula \\( P(n, k) = \\frac{n!}{(n-k)!} \\), where \\( n = 10 \\) and \\( k = 3 \\).\n\nHere is the step-by-step calculation:\n\n1. Identify the value of \\( n \\) and \\( k \\):\n   \\[\n   n = 10 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad k = 3\n   \\]\n\n2. Substitute \\( n \\) and \\( k \\) into the permutation formula:\n   \\[\n   P(10, 3) = \\frac{10!}{(10-3)!} = \\frac{10!}{7!}\n   \\]\n\n3. Simplify the factorials. Recall that \\( 10! = 10 \\times 9 \\times 8 \\times 7! \\):\n   \\[\n   P(10, 3) = \\frac{10 \\times 9 \\times 8 \\times 7!}{7!} = 10 \\times 9 \\times 8\n   \\]\n\n4. Perform the multiplication:\n   \\[\n   10 \\times 9 = 90\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   90 \\times 8 = 720\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the number of different settings possible is \\(\\boxed{720}\\).",
