      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To determine the number of different settings for a suitcase lock with 3 dials where all three digits must be different, we can follow these steps:\n\n1. **Identify the total number of options for each dial:**\n   Each dial has 10 possible digits (0 through 9).\n\n2. **Calculate the number of choices for the first dial:**\n   Since there are no restrictions on the first dial, it can be any of the 10 digits.\n   \\[\n   \\text{Number of choices for the first dial} = 10\n   \\]\n\n3. **Calculate the number of choices for the second dial:**\n   The second dial must be different from the first dial. Therefore, it can be any of the remaining 9 digits.\n   \\[\n   \\text{Number of choices for the second dial} = 9\n   \\]\n\n4. **Calculate the number of choices for the third dial:**\n   The third dial must be different from both the first and second dials. Therefore, it can be any of the remaining 8 digits.\n   \\[\n   \\text{Number of choices for the third dial} = 8\n   \\]\n\n5. **Calculate the total number of different settings:**\n   The total number of different settings is the product of the number of choices for each dial.\n   \\[\n   \\text{Total number of different settings} = 10 \\times 9 \\times 8\n   \\]\n\n6. **Perform the multiplication:**\n   \\[\n   10 \\times 9 = 90\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   90 \\times 8 = 720\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the total number of different settings possible is \\(\\boxed{720}\\).",
